Read a dialog's dlg_out list in next_state_dlg() under the list's own lock.

next_state_dlg() takes the head of dlg->dlg_entry_out before its switch,
holding only the dialog's hash entry lock, and dereferences it when a 2xx
arrives for a dialog already confirmed (the retransmission check against the
to-tag) or concurrently confirmed. The list is changed and its cells freed
under dlg->dlg_out_entries_lock (link_dlg_out(), dlg_remove_dlg_out(),
dlg_remove_dlg_out_tag()), by other SIP workers handling the same call's
other replies, so the cell can be gone by the time it is read. With a callee
that answers at once, 180 and 200 reach the S-CSCF together and are relayed
by different workers; at 300-400 calls/s the S-CSCF died within 20-120 s,
SIGSEGV in a UDP worker:

  next_state_dlg (event=DLG_EVENT_RPL2xx) dlg_hash.c, dlg_out->to_tag.len
  dlg_onreply dlg_handlers.c
  run_trans_callbacks_with_buf / relay_reply / reply_received

and the main process then shut kamailio down. Both comparisons now read
the head, and walk the list, inside dlg_out_entries_lock (taken after the
entry lock, the order destroy_dlg() already uses), and an empty list counts
as nothing to compare. The lock is not held across the whole function:
unref_dlg_unsafe() there can destroy the dialog, which takes it too.

